Native process-state workload
=============================

On 2.11BSD, from bsd/top:

    cc -O -i -o top-workload tests/workload.c
    ./top-workload 30

While it runs, use a second terminal to run the candidate top. The harness
prints the PIDs of its stopped child, intentional zombie, and churn manager.
The 33 sleeping children force top beyond its initial 32-row allocation.
The manager forks and reaps one short-lived child per second, exercising
process creation and exit while top samples the live kernel tables.

Check that top stays responsive, reports T and Z for the printed PIDs, and
does not interpret the zombie's overlaid accounting fields as segment sizes.
PID sorting (-o 0) helps locate the printed PIDs; rows may extend beyond one
screen. Repeat with idle filtering, and verify recovery after the workload
ends. Churn is intentionally transient and need not appear in every frame.

The default is 30 seconds; an optional duration accepts 5 through 300 seconds.
Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, SIGHUP, or normal expiry makes the parent kill and reap only
its recorded direct children, including the stopped child. A failed fork
also cleans up the partial workload. Do not SIGKILL the parent: no process
can run cleanup after that signal, and its stopped child would need manual
cleanup. No other users' processes or files are changed.

This is a native, explicitly launched integration test. It is not run by
tests/run.sh and is not a CPU benchmark: its purpose is lifecycle coverage.
